Selecting a couple of near-duplicate achievements on the **Experience** tab and asking
the assistant to sort them out did not work as well as it looked. The conversation opened
on the right achievements and the agent could not actually see any of them.

### What was going wrong

The opening message named your selected achievements by their internal ids. The assistant
had one way to look at your bank — a search that works by *meaning*, the same one it uses
to find evidence for "led a team" or "ran Kubernetes in production". Ids mean nothing to a
search like that, so it found nothing, and finding nothing is how it learns an achievement
does not exist.

So it searched again, more broadly, and eventually answered about a different set of
achievements — ones a text search happened to turn up. Everything it said was about work
you had done. It just was not about the ones you had picked.

### What it does now

The assistant can read achievements directly. When you select some and open the
interviewer, it reads those exact ones first — claim, situation, numbers, skills and the
role each belongs to — and opens by telling you what they say.

That changes what it can be trusted with:

- **Merges are proposed with evidence.** Before suggesting two achievements are the same
  work, it tells you what both actually say, so you can disagree. It used to be able to
  ask for a merge it had never examined.
- **Sharpening an achievement no longer risks the numbers already in it.** Metrics and
  skills are stored as a set, so an assistant adding one number without having read the
  rest could replace them. It reads first.
- **The duplicate clusters we detect are readable.** Your profile summary has always
  reported groups of near-paraphrases; the assistant can now open one and describe it
  rather than pointing at it.

### The panel stops competing with the list

The interviewer opens beside your bank rather than sending you to another page. It was
also sitting *inside* the page's content column, so the conversation and the achievements
it was about were both cramped into half the space.

The panel is now docked to the edge of the window and the page steps aside for it, with
the account menu folding to its icon rail while it is open. The result is that your
achievements are no wider apart than before — on most screens they get slightly more room
than with the panel closed. Your menu returns to however you had it when you close the
panel.

On narrower screens there is no arrangement where both are comfortable, so the
conversation covers the bank and hands it back when you close it, as it did before.

Nothing changed about the conversation itself: same interviewer, same tools, same session
list, and `/my/assistant` still opens it full-width if you would rather have the room.
